% ============================================================================
% ocean-zones — each of the ocean's first three depth zones as a NUMBER
% (1-3), the position in the ordered-by-depth sequence (adj-facts-stdlib,
% OCEANOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling library to the already-shipped `plant-life-cycle.adj` and
% `frog-life-cycle.adj` — the SAME plain numbered ordered-sequence recall
% shape, applied to a DIFFERENT domain. The ocean is layered by how deep
% sunlight reaches: the sunlight zone is shallowest and brightest, the
% twilight zone is next and dimmer, and the midnight zone is deeper still
% and fully dark. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? ocean_zone(twilight_zone, $N)   % 2
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `ocean_zone(zone, order)` carrying the source citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% position WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains
% on a zone that is not one of the three tabled here. The query also runs
% BACKWARD — bind a number and recall the zone:
%
%     ? ocean_zone($Z, 1)   % sunlight_zone
%
% The three zones IN ORDER, as a little truth table:
%
%     zone            order   quote (verbatim)
%     -------------   -----   --------------------------------------------------------------
%     sunlight_zone   1       "Because water strongly absorbs light, sunlight penetrates
%                               only to depths of about 200 meters (656 feet)."
%     twilight_zone   2       "It lies 200 to 1,000 meters below the ocean surface, just
%                               beyond the reach of sunlight."
%     midnight_zone   3       "The midnight zone, or bathypelagic, extends to about 4,000
%                               meters (about 13,100 feet), which reaches the ocean floor
%                               in many places is in perpetual darkness."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from the Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution
% (WHOI) "Ocean Zones" page, "What are the five ocean zones?" section,
% which lists all five zones (sunlit/twilight/midnight/abyssal/hadal) in
% depth order in one summary sentence, then gives each its own subsection
% in that same order — this table only tables the first three, the same
% way `plant-life-cycle.adj` deliberately stops at seedling rather than
% continuing to "flowering." WebFetch-verified before writing.
% `trust consensus` — WHOI is a reputable, long-running oceanographic
% research institution but is NOT a .gov domain, so this sits in the same
% tier as Ducksters/Grammarly/National Geographic elsewhere in this
% stdlib, distinct from the `authoritative` tier reserved for primary
% .gov sources like NOAA (see `ocean-observing-instruments.adj`, the only
% other library in this directory, which uses the `authoritative` tier
% for its NOAA citations). A zone that is NOT one of these three —
% `abyssal_zone`, a real deeper zone the source does name, but not one
% this table's three rows number — is deliberately NOT a row, so a
% recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a position. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table ocean_zone {
    columns zone, order

    row (sunlight_zone, 1)
    row (twilight_zone, 2)
    row (midnight_zone, 3)

    source "Because water strongly absorbs light, sunlight penetrates only to depths of about 200 meters (656 feet)."
    locator "https://www.whoi.edu/know-your-ocean/ocean-topics/how-the-ocean-works/ocean-zones/"
    trust consensus
}
